#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
bar-touch — a touch-native top bar, plus "mouse mode" for the laptop touchscreen.

TOUCH ON THE BAR (always on)
  Every waybar module's "on-click" is `bar-touch click <module>`. The real
  command lives in that module's "touch" object in config.jsonc (waybar ignores
  unknown keys). The daemon reads the touchscreen itself, so it knows whether a
  click came from a finger and how long the finger stayed down:
    * mouse click          -> run the command, exactly as before (no popup)
    * finger tap  (<0.5 s) -> run the command, then a popup under the finger
                              shows the module's state before -> after
    * long press (>=0.5 s) -> a card: what the module is, its full state, and a
                              big button for every action it has (tap,
                              right-click, middle-click, scroll up/down), so
                              right-click and scroll are reachable by finger
  Workspace buttons and tray icons are built into waybar and cannot be wrapped;
  they keep waybar's own touch behaviour (a tap activates).

ON-SCREEN KEYBOARD (the layout icon, hyprland/language; 2026-10-03)
  tap = show / hide a full keyboard along the bottom of the screen (windows shrink
  above it, focus stays where it was), typed through a uinput keyboard; its
  layout key switches every keyboard between the layouts in Hyprland's
  input:kb_layout, and the keycaps are labelled for the active layout (xkbcli).
  Long press = card. A touch outside it does NOT close it (unlike the popups).

MOUSE MODE (the hand icon; state in ~/.config/bar-touch/mouse-mode)
  The daemon grabs the touchscreen (EVIOCGRAB), so apps stop seeing touches,
  and turns them into pointer input:
    * tap            -> the arrow jumps to the finger and left-clicks
    * long press     -> the arrow jumps there and right-clicks (fires at 0.5 s)
    * drag           -> left-button drag, the arrow follows the finger
    * two fingers swiped up -> an overview of every open window; tap one to
                               switch to it, its ✕ to close it (also the
                               windows icon on the bar, in any mode)
  On the bar itself a touch holds the left button for as long as the finger
  is down, so bar taps and long presses behave the same in both modes.
  Stopping the daemon releases the grab (the kernel drops it with the fd).

TESTED FACTS (waybar 0.15.0, Hyprland, 2026-09-24)
  * A touch tap on a waybar module fires "on-click" ~6 ms after finger-down,
    and "on-click-release" at finger-up — so the verdict waits for the finger.
  * `hyprctl dispatch movecursor X Y` then a uinput BTN_LEFT lands on the
    surface under the new position.
  * A uinput multitouch device is picked up by Hyprland like a real screen;
    the tests drive the whole path with one (see docs/touch-bar.md).

Commands spawned by the daemon get the environment of the waybar process that
invoked the client (waybar's PATH/OMARCHY_PATH), and the unit uses
KillMode=process so restarting the daemon never kills apps it launched.

Usage:
  bar-touch daemon                  # the service (bar-touch.service)
  bar-touch click <module>          # waybar on-click
  bar-touch mouse [toggle|on|off]   # mouse mode
  bar-touch mouse-status            # waybar JSON for the hand icon
  bar-touch overview                # show all open windows
  bar-touch status                  # daemon diagnostics

Testing: BAR_TOUCH_DEVICES="name1,name2" limits the daemon to those input
devices, so a test instance can grab a virtual screen without the real one.
"""

